WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 8: Routines, pipelines, and integration — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.000
Software engineering 201. Lesson eight.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.320
Routines, pipelines, and integration.

3
00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.032
Quick question. It's two in the morning.

4
00:00:10.032 --> 00:00:11.922
A job charges a customer's card.

5
00:00:11.922 --> 00:00:16.647
The payment goes through, but the connection drops before the answer comes back.

6
00:00:16.647 --> 00:00:18.832
So the job tries again. What happens?

7
00:00:18.832 --> 00:00:21.136
Nothing? The customer is charged twice?

8
00:00:21.136 --> 00:00:23.439
Or the processor catches it on its own?

9
00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:25.211
Quite possibly, charged twice.

10
00:00:25.211 --> 00:00:28.932
Some processors catch a quick duplicate, but don't count on it.

11
00:00:28.932 --> 00:00:30.585
Design the retry to be safe.

12
00:00:30.585 --> 00:00:32.180
That design is this lesson.

13
00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:36.176
Connecting systems is the oldest problem in this series.

14
00:00:36.176 --> 00:00:39.497
Thirty years ago: overnight batch files, and EDI

15
00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:42.492
Then web services. Then an API for everything.

16
00:00:42.492 --> 00:00:49.720
Now, agents calling tools. And every one of those is still running somewhere, often inside the same enterprise.

17
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.825
A skill is one job, written down and tested, that runs when someone asks.

18
00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:57.300
But some work should start on its own.

19
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.514
On a schedule: every night at two.

20
00:00:59.514 --> 00:01:02.640
On an event: a payment fails, or a file arrives.

21
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.985
Or when the step before it finishes.

22
00:01:04.985 --> 00:01:07.460
Call a skill plus a trigger a routine.

23
00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.000
And routines, chained, make a pipeline.

24
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:15.129
Here's one, in our made-up subscription-billing system: the nightly billing run.

25
00:01:15.129 --> 00:01:17.339
On a schedule, find the renewals due.

26
00:01:17.339 --> 00:01:20.147
Charge each one, through the payment processor.

27
00:01:20.147 --> 00:01:23.791
Write the invoices. Send the export to the accounting system.

28
00:01:23.791 --> 00:01:28.450
And last, reconcile: does our ledger match what the processor says it charged?

29
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:36.049
Between every two steps, something crosses: the renewals due, the charge results, the invoices, the export file.

30
00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:38.249
Give each a clear, written format.

31
00:01:38.249 --> 00:01:42.650
Then any step can be rebuilt, or rerun, without touching the others.

32
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.328
And every step leaves a record: what came in, what went out, what failed.

33
00:01:47.328 --> 00:01:51.835
A thousand renewals due. Nine hundred eighty-eight charged, twelve declined.

34
00:01:51.835 --> 00:01:55.274
Nine hundred eighty-eight invoices, and as many rows sent.

35
00:01:55.274 --> 00:01:58.179
The numbers are made up, but they have to add up.

36
00:01:58.179 --> 00:02:02.330
When they don't, the run stops there, and you know which step to open.

37
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.950
Back to two A.M. Partway through the charge step, the connection drops.

38
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.656
One charge went out, and no answer came back.

39
00:02:09.656 --> 00:02:11.641
Did it go through? We don't know.

40
00:02:11.641 --> 00:02:14.107
So the retry has to be safe to run twice.

41
00:02:14.107 --> 00:02:16.933
Renewals already recorded as charged, it skips.

42
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:22.707
Every other one, it sends with a key that never changes: this subscription, this billing period.

43
00:02:22.707 --> 00:02:28.661
If the processor has already seen that key, it returns the first result, instead of charging again.

44
00:02:28.661 --> 00:02:32.690
That's an idempotency key, and many payment processors support one.

45
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.757
Last, reconcile compares our ledger with the processor's record.

46
00:02:36.757 --> 00:02:41.635
If they don't match, the pipeline doesn't guess, and doesn't fix the numbers itself.

47
00:02:41.635 --> 00:02:44.074
It keeps every record, and tells a person.

48
00:02:44.074 --> 00:02:46.107
Most of these steps are plain code.

49
00:02:46.107 --> 00:02:51.450
An agent earns its place where a step needs judgment, like working out why they don't match.

50
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.291
The same thinking covers everything you don't control.

51
00:02:55.291 --> 00:02:57.231
Take a partner's nightly file.

52
00:02:57.231 --> 00:02:59.752
Check it first. Late: wait, then alert.

53
00:02:59.752 --> 00:03:01.885
Twice: recognize it, and skip it.

54
00:03:01.885 --> 00:03:04.471
Broken: set it aside, and tell a person.

55
00:03:04.471 --> 00:03:07.704
You can't count on anything arriving exactly once.

56
00:03:07.704 --> 00:03:11.130
So design for at least once, and make twice harmless.

57
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.902
So, to recap. Give every hand-off a clear format.

58
00:03:14.902 --> 00:03:17.975
Make every step leave a record that adds up.

59
00:03:17.975 --> 00:03:20.349
Make every step safe to run twice.

60
00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:24.400
And when the numbers don't match, stop, and tell a person.

61
00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.352
Which gives us rule eight, for your agents.

62
00:03:27.352 --> 00:03:30.754
Before any work runs on its own, write down four things.

63
00:03:30.754 --> 00:03:33.548
What starts it. What crosses at each hand-off.

64
00:03:33.548 --> 00:03:35.310
What record each step leaves.

65
00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.347
And what happens when a step fails, or runs twice.

66
00:03:38.347 --> 00:03:41.020
No answer? It isn't ready to run unattended.

67
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:47.008
Now, your turn. Pick one job that already runs on its own: a nightly report, or a sync.

68
00:03:47.008 --> 00:03:52.516
Describe its steps to your AI, and ask, for each step: what happens if it runs twice?

69
00:03:52.516 --> 00:03:54.785
And what record would prove it ran?

70
00:03:54.785 --> 00:03:57.960
The steps with no good answer are where to start.

71
00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:02.825
A pipeline that runs on its own is already operating the system.

72
00:04:02.825 --> 00:04:05.082
So whose name does it run under?

73
00:04:05.082 --> 00:04:07.058
What is it allowed to touch?

74
00:04:07.058 --> 00:04:09.033
And who can see what it did?

75
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:13.477
That's the last lesson: agents that operate enterprise systems.

76
00:04:13.477 --> 00:04:15.100
See you in lesson nine.
